            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>Why Multi-Account Teams Need Tiered Management</h2>
<p>When a team expands from single to multiple ad accounts, common problems emerge: creatives撞车 across accounts, budget allocation based on intuition, high-value accounts accidentally paused, and inability to compare performance data across product lines. </p>
<p>The root cause is the lack of clear account tiering logic. Tiering isn't about ranking accounts by importance—it's about helping the team understand priorities, where to allocate resources, and who has permission to perform what operations.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Account Tiering Model: By Value and Risk</h2>
<h3>Tier 1 Core Accounts</h3>
<ul>
<li>Represent over 50% of total team budget</li>
<li>Main accounts with proven ROI models and stable volume</li>
<li>Linked payment methods with sufficient balance</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Management principle</strong>: Dedicated personnel, double-confirmation for operations, priority balance protection</p>
<h3>Tier 2 Growth Accounts</h3>
<ul>
<li>Testing new creatives, product lines, or markets</li>
<li>Daily budget typically 30-50% of Tier 1</li>
<li>Allows moderate aggressive bidding and scaling tests</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Management principle</strong>: Weekly performance reviews, hard spending caps, timely shutdown of underperforming creatives</p>
<h3>Tier 3 Backup/Isolation Accounts</h3>
<ul>
<li>Initial trial accounts for new platform onboarding</li>
<li>Emergency replacement accounts when facing bans</li>
<li>Small budget, not responsible for primary user acquisition</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Management principle</strong>: Maintain low balance, regular viability checks, documented account info for emergency activation</p>
<hr />
<h2>Implementation Checklist</h2>
<h3>Step 1: Audit Existing Account Inventory</h3>
<p>Document the following in a spreadsheet:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Field</th>
<th>Description</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Account Name</td>
<td>Internal code for identification</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Product Line</td>
<td>Corresponding product/brand</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Platform</td>
<td>Meta / TikTok / Google / etc.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Account Status</td>
<td>Active / Restricted / Suspended</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Tier Level</td>
<td>Tier 1/2/3</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Account Manager</td>
<td>Media buyer name</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Payment Method</td>
<td>Credit card / Agency / Top-up platform</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Alert Threshold</td>
<td>Balance level triggering notification</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Step 2: Establish Tier-Specific Operation Rules</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>New accounts must start in Tier 3</strong>, evaluated for upgrade after 7 days or $500 spend</li>
<li><strong>Any operation on Tier 1 accounts (budget changes, ad group pauses, audience adjustments) requires team notification</strong></li>
<li><strong>Tier 2 accounts auto-trigger notifications when daily spend exceeds threshold</strong></li>
<li><strong>Never share Tier 1 accounts across product lines</strong> to avoid data confusion</li>
</ul>
<h3>Step 3: Create Weekly Account Health Reports</h3>
<p>Fixed delivery every Friday, with these checks:</p>
<ul>
<li>Weekly spend totals and distribution by tier</li>
<li>Tier 1 account ROI trends (this week vs. last week)</li>
<li>Tier 2 new creative test data summary</li>
<li>Tier 3 survival rate (any bans?)</li>
<li>Budget adjustment plans for next week</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Daily Checklist: 5 Minutes to Prevent Disasters</h2>
<blockquote>
<p>Complete these checks at the start of each workday. Can be delegated to junior staff or interns.</p>
</blockquote>
<ol>
<li><strong>Balance check</strong>: Is Tier 1 balance below 3 days of spend?</li>
<li><strong>Status check</strong>: Are all accounts active, any accidental pauses?</li>
<li><strong>Budget check</strong>: Did yesterday's actual spend deviate more than ±20% from plan?</li>
<li><strong>Creative check</strong>: Any duplicate creatives running across same product line accounts?</li>
<li><strong>Notification check</strong>: Any missed critical operation alerts from previous day?</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<h2>Common Pitfalls and Solutions</h2>
<p><strong>Pitfall 1: All eggs in one basket</strong><br />
A utility app team concentrated 80% of budget in 2 Meta accounts. When one was banned for policy violation, GM dropped 40% overnight.</p>
<p><strong>Solution</strong>: Even with stable Tier 1 accounts, maintain at least 1 Tier 2 backup account and diversify across agencies or开户 channels.</p>
<p><strong>Pitfall 2: Tier 2 accounts lose control</strong><br />
A growth account performed well, team kept adding budget. After 3 months, Tier 2 became the actual main account but was still managed under test-phase rules.</p>
<p><strong>Solution</strong>: Evaluate Tier 2 spend ratio monthly. If it exceeds 35%, trigger an upgrade review and apply Tier 1 management standards.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Quick Start Action Items</h2>
<ol>
<li>Spend 30 minutes today filling out the account inventory spreadsheet</li>
<li>Align the team on three-tier definitions</li>
<li>Set balance alert notifications (recommend 7-day and 3-day thresholds)</li>
<li>Begin daily 5-minute checks next week</li>
</ol>
<p>Account tiering isn't a one-time project. Conduct quarterly tier reassessments and adjust resource allocation based on business changes.</p>
